================================================================================
THE FIRST COMMANDS AFTER RECOVERY, PRE-VERIFIED OFFLINE
zl1 (LeEco Pro3, MSM8996pro), Ubuntu Touch 24.04-2.x, 2026-09-23
OFFLINE ONLY: the device was in Qualcomm EDL (05c6:9008, port 3-3) for this whole
session. Nothing below ran on it. Three host-side harnesses, the real device
scripts under test, three real defects found.
================================================================================


0. WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The device has been in EDL, and the first boot after the long power press is
expensive: the pstore witness to the previous death is readable only on that
boot (the kmsg ring wraps in ~1 minute and a reboot destroys the rest), the
keeper-retirement verdict has to come from that boot's netwatch log, and if the
addresses do not come up there is no SSH and getting SSH back needs another
finger on the power button. A shell error in the first command wastes that boot.

So the scripts that run first -- and the one that needs a hand -- were
exercised on the host instead:

  scripts/device/zl1-edl-postmortem.sh       (health-check item 0, docs 86)
  scripts/device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh   (health-check item 0b, the gate that
                                              licenses item 0d, docs 88/94)
  scripts/device/zl1-orientation-axes.sh     (item 1b, docs 92 -- read-only, but the
                                              decisive run takes a person holding the
                                              phone still and upright, so a parser bug
                                              there wastes a human step too)

Both harnesses are committed, because the previous exercises of these two
scripts were one-off: docs 86 section 5 records that five synthetic cases were
run by hand, and docs 88 that four logs were, and neither could be re-run. Both
exercises had already found real bugs (86: a backtick that executed `reboot edl`;
88/94: a cmdline substring match that killed the test shell). Re-running them
found three more:

  1. every `--flag) V="$2"; shift 2` under `set -u` aborts the whole script when
     the flag is last -- `2: parameter not set` (dash), `$2: unbound variable`
     (bash). Five of the scripts in the recovery chain had one (section 2);
  2. zl1-boot-address-check.sh printed a stopped netwatch's state AND the
     "systemctl is not here" fallback, on two lines, because
     `systemctl is-active` prints "inactive" and exits non-zero (section 3);
  3. zl1-edl-postmortem.sh's fallback snapshot lookup never worked: `ls -tr`
     without `-d` lists the CONTENTS of the archive directories, so the next
     `ls` ran in the caller's working directory -- where, in the demonstration
     in section 4, it matched an unrelated host file. That path exists to find
     the driver's own probe-time line, which the script itself calls the most
     valuable thing it can find.

Nothing was written to the device, no partition, no boot image, no QDL/firehose
tool, and no reboot was attempted.


1. HOW THE THREE HARNESSES TEST THE REAL SCRIPTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
None of the three reimplements the logic it checks. Each copies the device script
with `sed`, rewriting only the OPERATIONAL occurrences of its device paths so
they point into a fake root, and then runs that copy:

  /proc/device-tree/model, /proc/uptime, /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id,
  /proc/config.gz, /sys/module/*/parameters/download_mode, /sys/kernel/dload,
  /sys/fs/pstore, /userdata/zl1-kmsg (D=), /sys/class/net/*, /etc/systemd/system

Paths that appear inside printed strings are left alone, so the report still
reads like the report. Each harness then verifies `sh -n` on the rewritten copy
and, for the address check, that its `D=`/`NETLOG=` rewrite actually applied --
a harness that silently tests an unpatched copy would be worse than none.

Three properties are checked per scenario, not one:

  * the verdict text (a substring the script itself prints) -- and for the
    post-mortem, also what it must NOT say;
  * the exit code (they carry meaning: address check 0 = netwatch-configured
    only; post-mortem 0 = ran, 1 = a witness was unavailable, 2 = not the zl1);
  * that NOTHING was executed and NOTHING was written. The post-mortem harness
    arms 23 stubs in a directory prepended to PATH -- reboot, poweroff, halt,
    qdl, QSaharaServer, fh_loader, firehose, fastboot, adb, dd, mount, umount,
    tee, mkfs.ext4, mkfs.vfat, systemctl, nsenter, lxc-start, lxc-stop, i2cset,
    i2cget, scp, ssh, flash -- each of which records its invocation into a
    marker file instead of acting. It then snapshots `find "$FR" -printf
    '%p %s %T@'` before and after and diffs it. "Read-only" is a claim; that is
    the check. It is aimed at this script in particular: its first version
    executed `reboot edl` while merely printing a report (docs 86 section 5).


2. THE DEFECT IN EVERY VALUE-TAKING FLAG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Reproduced on the host, in both shells, before the fix:

  $ sh scripts/device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh --log
  scripts/device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh: 42: 2: parameter not set
  rc=2
  $ bash scripts/device/zl1-thermal.sh --seconds
  scripts/device/zl1-thermal.sh: line 49: $2: unbound variable
  rc=1

`$2` is unbound when the flag is the last argument, and `set -u` turns that into
an exit -- so the message names the shell's own parameter ("2: parameter not
set") rather than the flag, and nothing in the script runs. On the device this
would be the whole first command doing nothing.

The fix is one idiom, `${2?message}` instead of `"$2"`, applied to every
value-taking flag in the recovery chain and verified one by one on the host:

| script (host path shown as `host/`) | flags |
|---|---|
| `device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh` | `--log` |
| `device/zl1-gps-probe.sh` | `--seconds` |
| `device/zl1-orientation-axes.sh` | `--seconds`, `--interval` |
| `device/zl1-thermal.sh` | `--seconds`, `--top`, `--hold` |
| `host/zl1-camera-app-test.sh` | `--seconds`, `--run-seconds`, `--extra-args`, `--outdir` |

It is deliberately `${2?...}` and not `${2:?...}`: a MISSING value now says what
is wrong, while an explicitly empty value (`--extra-args ""`) keeps behaving
exactly as it did. Verified, per flag: with no value -> a message naming the
flag and rc 2 (dash) / 1 (bash); with a value -> the parse still works and the
script proceeds; with `--log ""` -> no shell error at all (the address check
then reports the log as unreadable, which is the correct behaviour).


3. THE ADDRESS CHECK: SIX LOGS, TEN CHECKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The verdict logic is what licenses retiring the v63 debug keeper (docs 94), so
the harness pins all three verdicts plus the boot-boundary behaviour. The six
fixture logs are in the harness; what matters here is why two of them exist:

  * uptime RESETS each boot, and every netwatch log line begins with the uptime
    it was written at, so a two-boot log contains a large number followed by a
    small one. Only the `netwatch start` boundary separates them;
  * scenario C's newest boot logs no `ADDRS:` line and no heal, while an OLDER
    boot in the same file logs an `ADDRS:` line. Reading the wrong section would
    say "netwatch-configured" on a boot where the keeper did it -- the one
    direction of error that costs SSH;
  * scenario D is its mirror: the newest boot is clean and an older one healed.
    The older heal must not leak in and turn it into "heal-first".

Output of `sh scripts/host/zl1-boot-address-selftest.sh`:

  zl1 boot-address check -- offline self-test
    script under test: scripts/host/../device/zl1-boot-address-check.sh
    fake root:         /tmp/zl1-ba-selftest/fake
  
  == verdict logic ==
  PASS  A: the netwatch configured it, no heal -> netwatch-configured (exit 0)
  PASS  B: the addresses arrived only after a heal -> heal-first (exit 1)
  PASS  C: the newest boot says nothing (an older one did) -> inconclusive (exit 1)
  PASS  D: the newest boot is clean (an older one healed) -> netwatch-configured (exit 0)
  PASS  E: a heal and no addresses at all -> heal-first (exit 1)
  
  == the netwatch's own state line ==
  PASS  the process age comes from field 22 after stripping comm
  PASS  the unit state prints once (not twice when systemctl prints and fails)
  
  == the stale-install branch (an installed build without ensure_addrs) ==
  PASS  it says so and points at the reinstall
  
  == a flag with no value must not abort the shell ==
  PASS  '--log' with no value -> a message and a clean exit
  PASS  '--log ""' is handled without a shell error (it reports the log unreadable)
  
  pass=10 fail=0


  The same harness also pins the two smaller things found while writing it:
    * the netwatch process age must be computed from field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat AFTER
      stripping the parenthesised comm (docs 81's trap) -- uptime 412 with starttime 10000
      ticks must read 312 s;
    * the stale-install branch, i.e. an installed netwatch whose ensure_addrs() is gone.


4. THE POST-MORTEM: SEVEN DEVICE STATES, AND A FALLBACK THAT NEVER WORKED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs 86 section 5 records five cases run by hand, and two bugs they caught. The
committed harness runs seven states and checks 34 things; the seventh state is
the new one and it is the reason the paragraph above says "re-running". Two of
the seven are the same state reached two different ways:

  A  an oops in pstore, drain installed            -> FOUND (exit 0)
  B  pstore empty, the kmsg archive carries it      -> FOUND (exit 0)
  C  the driver's own probe line in THIS boot's live snapshot
                                                    -> "the hazard is inert" (exit 0)
  C2 the same line reachable ONLY through the keep/ fallback
                                                    -> the same verdict; this is
                                                       the scenario that failed
  D  armed, no witness anywhere                     -> "Still unattributed" (exit 0)
  E  download_mode = 0                              -> "not armed as compiled" (exit 0)
  F  no pstore directory and no kmsg directory      -> exit 1, and the verdict
                                                       still refuses to conclude
  G  not the zl1                                    -> exit 2, before reading
                                                       anything as ours

F is checked with `notwant` as well: it must not say FOUND, and it must not turn
an unavailable witness into an absence. That is the property docs 86 section 7
promised, and it is the property that decides whether a post-mortem is evidence
or a story.

C2 failed on the first run. The fallback is one line in section 1b:

    early="$(ls -tr "$D"/boot-*.log 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    [ -n "$early" ] || early="$(ls -tr "$K"/boot-*/ 2>/dev/null | while read -r d; do
                                 ls -tr "$d"*.log 2>/dev/null | head -1; done | head -1)"

`ls -tr "$K"/boot-*/` has no `-d`, so `ls` does not list the archive
DIRECTORIES: with one directory it lists that directory's contents, and with
several it prints `dir/:` headers first. Either way the loop body receives bare
file names instead of paths, and `ls -tr "boot-0002.log"*.log` then runs from the
CALLER'S working directory. Demonstrated (a throwaway tree, one file per
archive):

$ tree: keep/boot-aaaa/boot-1.log, keep/boot-bbbb/boot-2.log; cwd contains an unrelated *.log
$ ls -tr "$K"/boot-*/            # what the script did:
      /tmp/lsdemo/keep/boot-bbbb/:
      boot-2.log
      
      /tmp/lsdemo/keep/boot-aaaa/:
      boot-1.log
$ ls -dtr "$K"/boot-*/           # what it should be:
      /tmp/lsdemo/keep/boot-bbbb/
      /tmp/lsdemo/keep/boot-aaaa/
$ the loop as written  -> an unrelated file from the cwd (or nothing):
      v51-recovery-watch-20260611T095059Z.log
$ the loop with -dtr    -> the earliest archived snapshot, as intended:
      /tmp/lsdemo/keep/boot-bbbb/boot-2.log

Note what the as-written form returned: not an error, and not the archive -- an
unrelated `.log` that happens to live in the working directory here (a host-side
watch log). The exact name is not the point and it is not reproducible either:
`ls -tr` orders by mtime, so which stray file wins changes with the directory
contents and the clock. On the device, `early` would therefore be either empty
or an arbitrary log, and the four probe-message greps in section 1b would run
against the wrong file -- silently, since "not in that snapshot" is a
normal-looking line. Section 1b is where the script looks for the two lines it
calls the most valuable thing it can find ("Failed to set secure DLOAD mode"
downgrades the whole hazard from armed to inert).

This is the same bug family as docs 86 section 5's bug 2 (`ls -t` gave a
basename and the grep ran from the wrong directory): the fix then corrected
section 3 and left its sibling in the fallback. A one-off exercise does not find
that; a re-runnable harness does, on its second run.

The fix is the one character `-d`, with the reason written next to it, and the
harness now pins C (this boot's snapshot) and C2 (the archived one) separately
so the fallback cannot rot again unnoticed.

