4b. THE ORIENTATION PROBE: NO BUG, AND THAT IS ALSO A RESULT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A third harness was written for the same reason as the first two, and it found
nothing. `device/zl1-orientation-axes.sh` answers the user's "it keeps going
landscape" report (docs 70/91/92) and its AXES-SWAPPED / AXES-INVERTED verdict
is the only thing that licenses an accelerometer matrix trial; the decisive run
needs a person holding the phone still, upright in portrait, screen facing them,
for the whole window. So a parser bug there wastes a human step, not just a
command -- and the script is almost entirely parsers: it shells out to `gdbus`
and then pulls a uint32 out of one reply and three floats out of another with
sed/awk (`ov_of`, `xyz_of`, the |g| axis picker), and its verdict is a count of
the values that came out.

`host/zl1-orientation-axes-selftest.sh` puts a stub `gdbus` in front of PATH
(replies for loadPlugin, requestSensor and the two Properties.Get calls, with
the two property replies read from files so a scenario is one file write), and
checks the verdict, the exit code, and the table the human will read. 32 checks:

  * 4 with the g on y and --portrait-up      -> AXES-OK, exit 0;
  * 2 (and 1) with the g on x                -> AXES-SWAPPED, exit 3 -- the only
                                                verdict that licenses a change,
                                                so it is checked in both directions
                                                (`notwant AXES-SWAPPED` on the OK run);
  * 3 with the g on y                        -> AXES-INVERTED, exit 4;
  * 6 with --flat-up (g on z)                -> the documented flat conclusion,
                                                exit 0, and it may NOT say SWAPPED;
  * the same 6 with --portrait-up            -> exit 5, "Not upright";
  * an xyz reply that does not parse         -> exit 1, the guard that caught
                                                this script's first version;
  * punctuation drift (`(<uint64 N>, 4)`)    -> the ov_of fallback still reads 4;
  * no posture flag                          -> measurement only, exit 0, and no
                                                verdict is guessed;
  * the conf.d read                         -> the matrix line AND the adaptor
                                                line are both printed;
  * --explain                                -> prints the reversible procedure
                                                and runs no measurement;
  * `--seconds` with no value                -> a message, not a shell abort.

Every one passed on the first run. That is worth writing down as a result rather
than as an absence: it means the printed verdict will be about the phone's pose,
not about the parsing. It does NOT mean the verdict will be AXES-OK -- the
script's own header says the flat reading cannot decide anything (qtmir ignores
FaceUp/FaceDown by design), and the decisive run still needs the phone in a hand.

One small thing seen while writing it: `ts_of`/`age_of` are defined in the
script and `age_of` is never called, so the orientation reply's uint64 timestamp
is not displayed anywhere. Dead code, no behaviour to test, no bug.

